          "text": "{\n  \"target_identification\": {\n    \"speaker\": \"Goneril\",\n    \"addressee\": \"Lear\",\n    \"anchor_quote\": \"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"\n  },\n  \"target_translation\": \"I love you.\",\n  \"dimensions\": {\n    \"T\": {\n      \"score\": 4,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.99,\n      \"reason\": \"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's love for Lear as true. The rest of her turn intensifies the asserted degree of that love; whether the claim is sincere does not alter its clear avowal form.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"target\",\n          \"quotation\": \"I love you\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"P\": {\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"reason\": \"The expression answers Lear's request for a comparative declaration of filial love, but it does not itself create, renew, or invoke an obligation. Goneril compares her affection to that of a child and magnifies its value; she does not position herself as bound to future conduct or affirm specific filial duties. Lear's ensuing grant is his own act and does not convert her avowal into an undertaking.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"E\": {\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.95,\n      \"reason\": \"This is a solicited, elaborately constructed answer in a public contest, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Its extravagant intensity supports the rhetorical avowal but does not independently establish expressive-release force.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"O\": {\n      \"score\": 2,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"reason\": \"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions as a competitive answer and rhetorical bid for Lear's bounty. Lear has explicitly made the size of his gift depend on which daughter claims to love him most, so Goneril's superlative praise has a materially interested persuasive function not fully captured by T, P, or E.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"original_speech_situation\": \"In a public dynastic proceeding, Lear asks each daughter to say how much she loves him and announces that his largest bounty will follow the greatest claimed love. Goneril speaks first, addressing Lear with an extended, superlative declaration.\",\n  \"affection_evidence\": \"mixed\",\n  \"undertaking_and_uptake\": \"The supplied scene establishes no undertaking by Goneril in the focal expression: she avows and magnifies filial love without promising conduct or affirming herself as obligated. Lear accepts the answer for purposes of his contest and grants her territory, but that is uptake of her declaration as a successful answer, not acceptance of a promise. Goneril's actual affection remains uncertain: her avowal supplies some evidence, while the competitive reward structure and Cordelia's skeptical comparison give reason for doubt.\",\n  \"recognised_work\": true,\n  \"recognition_note\": \"The passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\n  \"limitations\": \"Only the supplied scene is used. It establishes the public incentive, the wording of Goneril's claim, Lear's immediate response, and Cordelia's nearby skepticism, but it does not conclusively establish Goneril's sincerity, private intentions, or later fulfilment of any familial expectations.\"\n}"
